{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SwQB6r9ytim9DwfL3Hjx6g","type":"tool_result","content":"or `render`. `setup` is the terminal link.\n_Avoid_: step, method (in prose)\n\n**Prop**:\nA named input declared with `withProps`. Inside `setup` it is a signal.\nAn attribute-backed prop is read from the host's HTML attribute of the same\nname; a property-only prop is set from JavaScript only. Every prop also has\na property accessor on the host: assigning a plain value writes the prop,\nand assigning a signal makes the prop read through to that signal. Once a\nsignal is assigned, the attribute is no longer updated and does not\nreflect the current value. A bound prop is read-only inside the\ncomponent: writing it from `setup` throws, so the component emits an\nevent instead.\n_Avoid_: attribute (that is the HTML side of a prop), input, option\n\n**Coercer**:\nA function that turns a raw attribute string, or its absence, into a typed\nprop value. The four builtins are `number`, `string`, `boolean`, `json`.\n## Hydration\n\nIn the [preceding serverside example](#serverside-example), the\nserver must know how to render the HTML that the client components depend on.\nThat means we have an unspecified dependency between HTML and JS.\nHydration is one way to render the correct HTML.\n\nYou can write a `.render` method, and then use a build tool\nto strip the `.render` method from the JS sent to the client. That way\nthe clientside bundle stays small, but on the server, every component can\nrender itself, and the components shipped to the client deal with behavior only.\n\nSee [`example-hydration/`](./example-hydration/) for a working example that\nuses Vite, and more docs.\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n## API\n\n### `define`\n\nFunction `define(tagName)` returns a [`ComponentBuilder`](#componentbuilder).\n\n```js\nfunction define (tagName:string):ComponentBuilder\n```\n\n\n#### `ComponentBuilder`\n\nThe object returned by [`define`](#define). Includes methods:\n\n| Method | Returns | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `.TAG` | `string` | The tag name passed to `define`. See [`.TAG`](#tag). |\n| `.withProps(fn)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Declare reactive attributes. See [Props](#props). |\n| `.withRefs(fn)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Declare DOM refs resolved once the element connects. See [Refs](#refs-1). |\n| `.withContexts(fn)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Consume context values published by an ancestor. See [`withContexts`](#withcontexts). |\n| `.render(fn, opts?)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Attach a server-render function. See [`.render`](#builderrenderfn-options) below. Not to be confused with the DOM helper [`microtags/render`](#microtagsrender). |\n| `.setup(fn)` | `MicrotagElementClass` | Registers the custom element and returns its class. See [`setup`](#setup) below. |\n\nMethods `withProps`, `withRefs`, and `withContexts` each return a new builder,\nso they chain in any order; `setup` ends the chain by registering the element.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\n### `builder.withProps`\n77:signal per JSON value (`route`, `count`, `noticeOpen`, `labels`), and\n107:  and `Notice.render` and `Badge.render` are still functions.\n115:  emit `increment` and `decrement`. Its `.render` nests a `Badge`, and\n116:  its setup binds a derived value to that badge's `label` prop. Its\n118:- `badge.ts` -- shows its `label` prop. Kept with `{ client: true }`\n271:go inline as `<${Badge} ... />`. `components/counter.ts` is written\n281:import { Badge } from './badge.js'\n294:            <${Badge} label=${String(props.start)} />\n309:    <hydra-badge label=\"5\"><span>5</span></hydra-badge>\n320:`Badge` inline in its `html` template. The badge also carries the","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2610be84-750a-4596-acbd-ed9ce3c7ac4d","uuid":"1d6d0858-8cbe-4739-baaf-2a369eba4653","timestamp":"2026-10-15T04:29:57.813Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"or `render`. `setup` is the terminal link.\n_Avoid_: step, method (in prose)\n\n**Prop**:\nA named input declared with `withProps`. Inside `setup` it is a signal.\nAn attribute-backed prop is read from the host's HTML attribute of the same\nname; a property-only prop is set from JavaScript only. Every prop also has\na property accessor on the host: assigning a plain value writes the prop,\nand assigning a signal makes the prop read through to that signal. Once a\nsignal is assigned, the attribute is no longer updated and does not\nreflect the current value. A bound prop is read-only inside the\ncomponent: writing it from `setup` throws, so the component emits an\nevent instead.\n_Avoid_: attribute (that is the HTML side of a prop), input, option\n\n**Coercer**:\nA function that turns a raw attribute string, or its absence, into a typed\nprop value. The four builtins are `number`, `string`, `boolean`, `json`.\n## Hydration\n\nIn the [preceding serverside example](#serverside-example), the\nserver must know how to render the HTML that the client components depend on.\nThat means we have an unspecified dependency between HTML and JS.\nHydration is one way to render the correct HTML.\n\nYou can write a `.render` method, and then use a build tool\nto strip the `.render` method from the JS sent to the client. That way\nthe clientside bundle stays small, but on the server, every component can\nrender itself, and the components shipped to the client deal with behavior only.\n\nSee [`example-hydration/`](./example-hydration/) for a working example that\nuses Vite, and more docs.\n\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n## API\n\n### `define`\n\nFunction `define(tagName)` returns a [`ComponentBuilder`](#componentbuilder).\n\n```js\nfunction define (tagName:string):ComponentBuilder\n```\n\n\n#### `ComponentBuilder`\n\nThe object returned by [`define`](#define). Includes methods:\n\n| Method | Returns | Description |\n|--------|---------|-------------|\n| `.TAG` | `string` | The tag name passed to `define`. See [`.TAG`](#tag). |\n| `.withProps(fn)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Declare reactive attributes. See [Props](#props). |\n| `.withRefs(fn)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Declare DOM refs resolved once the element connects. See [Refs](#refs-1). |\n| `.withContexts(fn)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Consume context values published by an ancestor. See [`withContexts`](#withcontexts). |\n| `.render(fn, opts?)` | `ComponentBuilder` | Attach a server-render function. See [`.render`](#builderrenderfn-options) below. Not to be confused with the DOM helper [`microtags/render`](#microtagsrender). |\n| `.setup(fn)` | `MicrotagElementClass` | Registers the custom element and returns its class. See [`setup`](#setup) below. |\n\nMethods `withProps`, `withRefs`, and `withContexts` each return a new builder,\nso they chain in any order; `setup` ends the chain by registering the element.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------\n\n### `builder.withProps`\n77:signal per JSON value (`route`, `count`, `noticeOpen`, `labels`), and\n107:  and `Notice.render` and `Badge.render` are still functions.\n115:  emit `increment` and `decrement`. Its `.render` nests a `Badge`, and\n116:  its setup binds a derived value to that badge's `label` prop. Its\n118:- `badge.ts` -- shows its `label` prop. Kept with `{ client: true }`\n271:go inline as `<${Badge} ... />`. `components/counter.ts` is written\n281:import { Badge } from './badge.js'\n294:            <${Badge} label=${String(props.start)} />\n309:    <hydra-badge label=\"5\"><span>5</span></hydra-badge>\n320:`Badge` inline in its `html` template. The badge also carries the","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SwQB6r9ytim9DwfL3Hjx6g","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
